  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/....\nThe main concern for next week remains the building heat as the \nsubtropical ridge gradually shifts westward back over our region \nbeginning Sunday. H5 heights are expected to climb from 588-591dm on \nSaturday to 594-596dm Sunday before peaking on Tuesday at around 596-\n597dm. Forecast H5 heights Monday through at least Wednesday fall \nclose to the 97th percentile of climatology for the period, but \nforecast highs remain at least a few degrees below records. An \nExtreme Heat Watch remains in effect for Monday-Wednesday for the \nsouth-central Arizona lower deserts and its very plausible it may \nneed to go through the rest of the workweek as the ridge is not \nlikely to weaken or move much late next week. The latest NBM \nforecast temperatures for late next week have also been trending up, \nbasically keeping highs around 110 degrees. The main question about \nthe heat will be the amount of convection that may form over eastern \nArizona potentially bring outflows into the lower deserts and/or \ndebris clouds. Guidance does agree some modest moisture advection is \nexpected early next week, but it should only be enough to help \nhigher terrain areas. As of right now, there are no clear signs the \nconvective potential will be enough to greatly affect temperatures \nearly next week, so more than likely the current forecast highs of \naround 110 degrees will hold.",
